messages.content:
A 24.9 mm sport rocket, 0.50 m long, on an Estes C6-5 with motor ejection and a 0.30 m chute, clears your 200 m floor easily: 353.4 m.
0.0498 kg liftoff, 9.8:1 thrust-to-weight, rail exit 26.3 m/s, landing 3.3 m/s, margin 2.90 cal — first draft passed every gate.
Choose the 5 s or 7 s delay after weighing the finished model; the charge fires at 6.9 s here, just after a 7.15 s apogee.
<design>
{
  "name": "24.9 mm C6-5 sport rocket",
  "stages": [
    {
      "name": "Sustainer",
      "components": [
        {"type": "nosecone", "shape": "ogive", "length_m": 0.1, "diameter_m": 0.0249, "wall_thickness_m": 0.0015, "material": "Polystyrene", "shoulder_length_m": 0.025},
        {"type": "bodytube", "name": "Body", "length_m": 0.4, "outer_diameter_m": 0.0249, "wall_thickness_m": 0.0005, "material": "Cardboard",
         "contents": [
           {"type": "parachute", "diameter_m": 0.3, "cd": 0.8, "deploy_event": "ejection", "material": "Polyethylene (thin)", "position_from_top_m": 0.03},
           {"type": "motor_mount", "outer_diameter_m": 0.0187, "length_m": 0.07, "wall_thickness_m": 0.00035, "material": "Cardboard",
            "motor": {"manufacturer": "Estes", "designation": "C6", "delay_s": 5.0},
            "overhang_m": 0.004, "centering_ring_count": 2, "centering_ring_material": "Cardboard"},
           {"type": "trapezoidal_fins", "count": 3, "root_chord_m": 0.055, "tip_chord_m": 0.022, "span_m": 0.038, "sweep_m": 0.022, "thickness_m": 0.0016, "material": "Balsa", "offset_from_aft_m": 0.0}
         ]}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
</design>

A 101.6 mm rocket on the Cesaroni N1100 reaches 9,052.4 m, about 29,700 ft, so 30,000 ft is met within about 1%.
15.4 kg liftoff, 7.9:1 thrust-to-weight, rail exit 21.0 m/s, landing 4.1 m/s on a 4.0 m chute, margin 1.65 cal.
Two fin sets bracketed the target: root 0.30 m simulated at 9,786 m and root 0.35 m at 8,388 m, so the design uses root 0.32 m, which simulated at 9,052 m. Warning: the simulator says body calculations may not be accurate at supersonic speeds (peak Mach 1.63). The tool labels the N1100 as N class, but 14,003 N*s falls in the O range by my recall, so confirm the class and certification level before flying. The deployment is 9.5 m/s, so use a full rail and a proper harness.
